# short-description: MedPlatform A/B layout for STM32MP2 (TF-A/FIP boot chain)
# long-description:
# The STM32MP257 counterpart of med-partitions.wks. Same A/B *policy* - two
# identical rootfs slots plus a /data volume no update touches - carried by the
# boot chain this SoC's ROM code actually requires.
#
#  +--------+--------+-----------+-----------+-------+-------+--------+------------+------------+----------+
#  | fsbla1 | fsbla2 | metadata1 | metadata2 | fip-a | fip-b | u-boot | med-boot   | med-root-a | med-root-b | med-data |
#  | TF-A   | TF-A   |           |           |       |       |  -env  | kernel+dtb | (active)   | (inactive) | LUKS     |
#  +--------+--------+-----------+-----------+-------+-------+--------+------------+------------+------------+----------+
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# med-partitions-efi.wks - then called med-partitions.wks, and then living in
# meta-med-distro - said that vendor BSPs needing TF-A/U-Boot at fixed offsets
# "ship their own WKS_FILE from the machine configuration, and med-os.conf sets
# WKS_FILE with '?=' so the BSP wins". For meta-st-stm32mp that is false, and
# the assumption cost a build: stm32mp25-disco.conf sets WKS_FILE_DEPENDS but
# leaves WKS_FILE itself commented out ("#WKS_FILE += ${OPTEE_WIC_FILE}"),
# offering sdcard-stm32mp257f-dk-optee-example.wks.in only as an example to
# copy. So the distro default won by forfeit, and do_image_wic tried to build
# an EFI/GRUB ESP on a board that has no ESP - failing on the one artefact that
# happens not to exist under that name, the kernel:
#
#   install: cannot stat '.../stm32mp25-disco/Image.gz': No such file or directory
#
# (meta-st deploys the kernel to ${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/kernel/, not to its root.
# That missing file was the symptom; the wrong layout was the defect.)
#
# This file names a board in almost every line, which is exactly why
# meta-med-bsp exists: nothing in meta-med-distro, meta-med-framework or
# meta-med-app may do that. Selection is by machine override on
# MED_WKS_FILE_DEFAULT in this layer's conf/layer.conf - the board fact and the
# board file are declared in the same layer, and the KAS project file only has
# to name the machine.
#
# What is NOT solved here
# -----------------------
# Slot selection *is* now implemented, but not in this file: the bootcmd in
# med-uboot-env-image reads BOOT_ORDER/BOOT_<slot>_LEFT and builds
# root=PARTUUID= from the two --uuid values below. The extlinux.conf the BSP
# generates still hardcodes slot A, deliberately - it is the rescue path for a
# lost or corrupt environment, and the one thing that must not change is that
# losing the environment degrades to booting slot A rather than to a panic.
#
# What remains unsolved here is the kernel. med-boot is shared by both slots and
# Image.gz lives on it, so an update replaces a rootfs and never a kernel; with
# CONFIG_MODVERSIONS=y a bundle carrying a different kernel version would boot
# and then fail at module load, silently. Fixing that is a change to this file -
# kernel inside each slot, or med-boot itself A/B - and is not attempted yet.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot chain. Offsets and part-types are the SoC's contract, not a preference:
# the ROM code searches GPT for these partition *names*, in this order, and
# fsbla1 must start at 17 KiB. Everything in this block is copied from
# meta-st-stm32mp/wic/sdcard-stm32mp257f-dk-optee-example.wks.in, deliberately
# verbatim - a "cleanup" here is a brick.
#
# The -ca35tdcid-ostl variant is used, and that choice is now backed by a boot
# on hardware rather than by ST's example .wks, which names the plain
# stm32mp257f-dk files. Three reasons, in order of weight:
#
#  1. it is what this machine declares: stm32mp25-disco.conf sets
#     EXTERNAL_DT_ENABLED = "1" and EXTERNAL_DEVICETREE_SDCARD =
#     "stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl". ST's example .wks is the legacy path;
#  2. it booted. Observed: "Board: stm32mp2 (st,stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl)"
#     and U-Boot loading /stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl.dtb by itself, because
#     it derives fdtfile from the devicetree built into the FIP - so changing
#     these four lines is enough, the kernel DTB follows;
#  3. M33_BOARDS_START_AT_STARTUP only includes the Cortex-M33 in this variant,
#     and the M33 is the whole AMP path the EEG needs next.
#
# What it did NOT fix, and this is worth recording so nobody re-runs the
# experiment: the OP-TEE panic in clk_stm32_pll_init survived the switch
# unchanged. The cause was the GPU (see MACHINE_FEATURES nogpu in layer.conf),
# not the devicetree variant.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# FSBL, aka TF-A BL2. Two copies: the ROM falls back to the second.
part fsbla1 --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --part-name=fsbla1 --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/arm-trusted-firmware/tf-a-stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl-optee-sdcard.stm32" --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 0x8301 --fixed-size 256K --align 17
part fsbla2 --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --part-name=fsbla2 --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/arm-trusted-firmware/tf-a-stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl-optee-sdcard.stm32" --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 0x8301 --fixed-size 256K

# TF-A firmware-update metadata: which FIP the ROM tries, and its try counter.
# This is the SoC's own A/B mechanism for the *bootloader*, one layer below the
# rootfs A/B this file's slots implement.
part metadata1 --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --part-name=metadata1 --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/arm-trusted-firmware/metadata.bin" --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 0x8301 --fixed-size 256K
part metadata2 --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --part-name=metadata2 --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/arm-trusted-firmware/metadata.bin" --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 0x8301 --fixed-size 256K

# FIP: OP-TEE + U-Boot. Two copies, same reason. The --uuid values are fixed
# because the metadata above refers to the FIP slots by UUID.
part fip-a --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --part-name=fip-a --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/fip/fip-stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl-optee-sdcard.bin" --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 19d5df83-11b0-457b-be2c-7559c13142a5 --fixed-size 4096K --uuid 4fd84c93-54ef-463f-a7ef-ae25ff887087
part fip-b --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --part-name=fip-b --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/fip/fip-stm32mp257f-dk-ca35tdcid-ostl-optee-sdcard.bin" --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 19d5df83-11b0-457b-be2c-7559c13142a5 --fixed-size 4096K --uuid 09c54952-d5bf-45af-acee-335303766fb3

# U-Boot environment. Created empty, and it is where RAUC's uboot backend reads
# and writes BOOT_ORDER. Two different consumers reach it two different ways,
# and both are satisfied here:
#
#   U-Boot itself, by GPT partition *name*. Its devicetree carries
#   u-boot,mmc-env-partition = "u-boot-env", and env/mmc.c looks that name up on
#   the MMC device it booted from - so the name below is an interface, exactly
#   like med-root-a/b.
#
#   fw_printenv/fw_setenv under Linux, by *PARTUUID*, through the
#   /etc/fw_env.config that meta-med-bsp's med-uboot-env-config writes. The UUID
#   below is declared once in conf/layer.conf as MED_UBOOT_ENV_PARTUUID and
#   substituted into both places, so the partition table and the address of the
#   partition cannot disagree.
#
# The --uuid is not decoration, and the reason is stronger than it first looked.
# The factory OpenSTLinux image on this board's eMMC carries partitions named
# bootfs, rootfs, vendorfs, u-boot-env, fip-a and fip-b, and
# /dev/disk/by-partlabel/ is one flat namespace across every block device: udev
# gives each colliding name to whichever disk enumerated first. Which disk that
# is was measured on two boots of this same hardware, with no configuration
# change between them, and came out differently: SD=mmcblk1/eMMC=mmcblk0 once,
# SD=mmcblk0/eMMC=mmcblk2 the next. Enumeration is a race, so by-partlabel is
# not merely "usually the eMMC" - it has no defined answer at all. And for this
# partition the answer measured on 2026-08-18 is the wrong disk:
# by-partlabel/u-boot-env resolved to /dev/mmcblk2p5, the factory eMMC, while
# ours is /dev/mmcblk0p7 by PARTUUID. Addressing it by name would have sent
# fw_setenv to another operating system's bootloader environment.
# Created empty until 2026-08-30, which was itself a defect: nothing wrote
# BOOT_ORDER at provisioning time, RAUC only writes it during an install, and
# "rauc status mark-good" writes only the attempt counter - so a freshly
# flashed device reported every slot bad, including the one it was running
# from, and had no primary slot. See docs/implementation_plan_uboot_ab.md.
#
# It now carries a built environment: the A/B bootcmd, BOOT_ORDER and the
# counters. The image is built from U-Boot's own u-boot-initial-env, because
# U-Boot *replaces* its built-in environment with a valid stored one rather
# than merging - a partial image would produce a board with no distro_bootcmd
# and no kernel_addr_r.
part u-boot-env --source rawcopy --fstype=ext4 --fsoptions "noauto" --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/med-uboot-env.bin" --part-name=u-boot-env --ondisk mmcblk --part-type 0x8301 --fixed-size ${MED_UBOOT_ENV_PART_SIZE_K}K --uuid ${MED_UBOOT_ENV_PARTUUID}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot filesystem: kernel, devicetrees and extlinux.conf, all of which the BSP
# has already installed into ${IMAGE_ROOTFS}/boot (kernel-image-image.gz,
# kernel-devicetree and stm32mp-extlinux). U-Boot's distro boot finds
# /extlinux/extlinux.conf on the active partition.
#
# Shared between both rootfs slots, which is the limitation named at the top of
# this file: an update writes med-root-b but the kernel it boots with comes
# from here. Sizing follows ST's example at 64 MiB against a ~12 MiB payload.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Named med-boot, not bootfs, and the rename is load-bearing. This board has an
# eMMC carrying a factory OpenSTLinux image whose partitions are named bootfs,
# rootfs, vendorfs, u-boot-env, fip-a, fip-b, metadata1 and metadata2 - the same
# names this layout uses. /dev/disk/by-partlabel/ is a flat namespace across all
# block devices, so the eMMC won every colliding name: observed on the device,
# by-partlabel/bootfs resolved to mmcblk0p6 while ours was mmcblk1p8.
#
# That mattered because this partition is the key store: med-data-provision.sh
# would have written the /data volume key onto the factory eMMC instead of the
# card. It would even have appeared to work, until the card was moved to another
# board and /data could no longer be decrypted - the exact failure the key
# custody design exists to prevent, reached by a route nobody had imagined.
#
# The names that cannot be renamed are fsbla1, fsbla2, metadata1, metadata2,
# fip-a and fip-b: the SoC ROM code and TF-A look those up by name. Nothing
# looks this one up by name - U-Boot finds it by number ("Scanning mmc 0:8" in
# the boot log) - so it is free to be unique.
part /boot --source rootfs --rootfs-dir=${IMAGE_ROOTFS}/boot --part-name=med-boot --label med-boot --ondisk mmcblk --fstype=ext4 --active --fixed-size 64M --extra-space 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The A/B slots. Everything below is the same policy as med-partitions.wks,
# and for the same reasons - see that file for why --fixed-size rather than
# --size (wic multiplies --size by 1.3 only on a partition that has a
# --source, which silently produced asymmetric slots) and why the GPT
# partition names are the only thing rauc-conf and crypttab ever refer to.
#
# --uuid is the one addition. The BSP generates extlinux.conf with
# root=PARTUUID=${DEVICE_PARTUUID_ROOTFS:SDCARD}, which for mmc0 is the
# constant below; a slot A with any other PARTUUID produces a kernel that
# panics with no root filesystem. Slot B has no such constraint yet precisely
# because nothing boots it yet.
part / --source rootfs --part-name med-root-a --label med-root-a --ondisk mmcblk --fstype=ext4 --align 1024 --fixed-size 1024 --uuid ${MED_ROOT_A_PARTUUID}

# Written by RAUC, never by the build. Identical in size to slot A is a hard
# requirement: a bundle built from a larger slot A does not fit.
#
# --uuid was deliberately absent here, with the note that "slot B has no such
# constraint yet precisely because nothing boots it yet". Booting slot B by hand
# on 2026-08-30 ended that, and the note was wrong within the hour: wic
# randomises an unspecified --uuid on every image build, so the bootcmd's
# root=PARTUUID= for slot B would name a partition that stops existing at the
# next build. Fixed values on both slots, declared together in layer.conf.
part --part-name med-root-b --label med-root-b --ondisk mmcblk --fstype=ext4 --align 1024 --fixed-size 1024 --uuid ${MED_ROOT_B_PARTUUID}

# LUKS container for patient data. Created as plain ext4 and converted on first
# boot by med-data-provision.service - on this machine with
# MED_DATA_KEY_SOURCE = "tpm2", which refuses to provision rather than falling
# back to a key that is not one.
part --part-name med-data --label med-data --ondisk mmcblk --fstype=ext4 --align 1024 --size 512

bootloader --ptable gpt
